    "text": "# Appointment Reminder Service\n\n## Scope\n\nA small service that, given an appointment (patient, provider, start time, channel), delivers a reminder at a configured lead time (e.g., 24h and 1h before). Assume SMS/email via a provider API. Assume a Postgres database and a pool of workers.\n\n## Data model\n\n```\nappointments(id, patient_id, start_at_utc, tz, status, version)\nreminders(id, appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc, state, attempt, next_attempt_at,\n          lease_until, provider_msg_id, sent_at, idem_key UNIQUE, updated_at)\n```\n\n- `start_at_utc` is stored in UTC; `tz` is the IANA zone of the appointment (patient's local zone).\n- `fire_at_utc` is computed once at scheduling time from `start_at_utc - lead_time`. **Do not** recompute from local wall-clock at send time \u2014 that's how DST bugs happen.\n- `idem_key` is deterministic: `sha256(appointment_id || channel || lead_time_bucket)`. This is the anchor for dedupe.\n- `state \u2208 {scheduled, leased, sent, cancelled, failed, dead}`.\n\n## Scheduling and time zones\n\n- All scheduling math is in UTC. The only place a time zone matters is (a) computing `fire_at_utc` from the appointment's local time, and (b) rendering the human-readable time in the message body.\n- DST edge cases: if an appointment is at 2:30 AM local on a spring-forward day, that wall time doesn't exist. Policy: reject at booking time, or shift to 3:00 AM \u2014 pick one and document it. Fall-back (ambiguous 1:30 AM)",
